>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `split_ss`

``` hol4
simpLib.split_ss : ssfrag
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Provides the simplifier's rule-directed case splitter.

`split_ss` is a named simpset fragment with the name `"split"`. Adding it
to a simpset installs the looper named `"splitter"`. Whenever that
looper runs, it builds its rule list from two sources:

1. the persistent theorems registered with the `[split]` attribute; and
2. cached split and assumption-split rules from `TypeBase` for datatype
   case constants occurring in the current conclusion or assumptions.

The looper invokes [`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC), so each
successful round performs one split and prefers the conclusion to the
assumptions. The simplification tactic then recursively processes the
resulting goals. A simpset bound installed by `simpLib.limit` bounds
successful looper rounds, including splitter rounds.

The fragment also contains an ordinary rewrite which collapses a
vacuous conditional split:

``` hol4
(b ==> t) /\ (~b ==> t) = t
```

Loopers are part of the simplification *tactic* pipeline. Thus tactics
built by `GEN_SIMP_TAC`, including `SIMP_TAC`, `ASM_SIMP_TAC`, and
`FULL_SIMP_TAC`, run the splitter. `SIMP_CONV` and `SIMP_RULE` do not run
loopers, although they can still use the fragment's ordinary rewrite.

Persistent rules have strategy names `split thy$name` or
`split_asm thy$name`. A datatype rule can be suppressed for one
invocation with either its full name `Excl "split.case thy$tyop"` or
its short name, for example `Excl "split.case bool"`.

### Failure

Constructing or adding `split_ss` never fails. If no rule applies, the
looper makes no change and ordinary simplification continues.

### Example

This static example equips `bool_ss` with datatype splitting:

``` hol4
val case_ss = bool_ss ++ simpLib.split_ss;

(* A SIMP_TAC using case_ss can change

     P (if b then x else y)

   to

     (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
*)
```

Custom persistent rules can be added at theorem declaration time:

``` hol4
Theorem pick_split[split]:
  !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
      (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
Proof
  ...
QED
```

